887939453125, 0.00649261474609375, 0.00812530517578125, -0.023651123046875, 0.019805908203125, 0.040008544921875, 0.00502777099609375, 0.016387939453125, 0.01885986328125, 0.02630615234375, 0.002994537353515625, -0.0040283203125, -0.038604736328125, 0.00693511962890625, -0.005096435546875, -0.01522064208984375, 0.019378662109375, -0.006275177001953125, 0.003528594970703125, -0.01319122314453125, 0.0034427642822265625, -0.00734710693359375, -0.019561767578125, 0.0209503173828125, 0.0185699462890625, 0.0009226799011230469, -0.019744873046875, -0.00897216796875, -0.014678955078125, -0.00461578369140625, 0.0099639892578125, 0.006877899169921875, -0.0093536376953125, 0.03271484375, -0.015777587890625, -0.015716552734375, -0.0078887939453125, -0.01384735107421875, 0.0269927978515625, -0.002468109130859375, 0.0209503173828125, -0.0304107666015625, 0.0142364501953125, -0.0292816162109375, -0.006542205810546875, 0.01019287109375, -0.0084686279296875, -0.005954742431640625, -0.0023517608642578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Permanental processes are a class of stochastic point process models that have found applications in spatial statistics and machine learning, particularly for modeling repulsion between points (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). In many real-world scenarios\u2014such as modeling the locations of trees in a forest, the distribution of disease cases in epidemiology, or the placement of wireless sensors\u2014there are known covariate effects (e.g., environmental gradients, spatial trends, or other features) that should be incorporated into the model for interpretability and improved predictive performance. Standard permanental process models, however, typically do not accommodate fixed effects, limiting their ability to leverage such domain knowledge. For example, if tree density varies systematically with elevation or soil type, a model without fixed effects cannot distinguish between spatial structure due to covariates and that due to latent repulsion. This motivates our extension of the permanental process to include fixed effects, enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information alongside nonparametric spatial structure.\n\n**Background and Model Formulation**\n\nA permanental process is a type of Cox process where the intensity function is a squared Gaussian process: for a domain \\( \\mathcal{X} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\), the intensity is \\( \\lambda(x) = \\phi^2(x) \\), where \\( \\phi \\) is a mean-zero Gaussian process with covariance kernel \\( k \\). The resulting point process exhibits repulsion, as points are less likely to cluster than in a Poisson process. This is in contrast to determinantal point processes (DPPs), which also model repulsion but are based on determinants rather than permanents, leading to different tractable properties and dependence structures (Lavancier et al., 2015). Log-Gaussian Cox processes (LGCPs) instead model the log-intensity as a Gaussian process, allowing for both clustering and repulsion depending on the covariance structure (M\u00f8ller et al., 1998; Diggle et al., 2013).\n\nLet \\( k: \\mathcal{X} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\) be a continuous, symmetric, positive definite kernel, and let \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\) denote the associated Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS). We denote by \\( z(x) = (z_1(x), \\ldots, z_p(x))^\\top \\) a vector of known covariates at location \\( x \\). Our main contribution is to extend the permanental process model to an intensity function of the form\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) are fixed effect coefficients and \\( f \\in \\mathcal{H}_k \\) is a nonparametric spatial effect. This formulation allows for direct incorporation of interpretable covariate effects, addressing a key limitation of previous models (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006).\n\n**Identifiability: Theory and Practical Diagnostics**\n\nIntroducing fixed effects raises identifiability concerns: the model parameters \\( (\\beta, f) \\) are not uniquely determined if the covariate functions \\( z_j \\) lie in the RKHS \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\). Formally, let \\( \\mathcal{Z} = \\text{span}\\{z_j : x \\mapsto z_j(x),\\ j=1,\\ldots,p\\} \\). Identifiability holds if and only if \\( \\mathcal{Z} \\cap \\mathcal{H}_k = \\{0\\} \\); otherwise, for any \\( h \\in \\mathcal{Z} \\cap \\mathcal{H}_k \\), the transformation \\( (\\beta, f) \\mapsto (\\beta + \\gamma, f - \\gamma^\\top z(x)) \\) leaves the model unchanged for \\( \\gamma \\) such that \\( \\gamma^\\top z(x) = h(x) \\).\n\n**Practical Algorithm for Checking Identifiability:**\n1. Compute the Gram matrix \\( G_z \\) of the covariates \\( z(x) \\) on observed data points.\n2. Compute the kernel matrix \\( K \\) for the same points.\n3. For each covariate \\( z_j \\), regress \\( z_j \\) onto the columns of \\( K \\) (e.g., via least squares or canonical correlation analysis).\n4. If any covariate is well-approximated by a linear combination of kernel features (high \\( R^2 \\)), identifiability is compromised.\n5. If non-identifiability is detected, orthogonalize the covariates with respect to the kernel features (e.g., Gram-Schmidt procedure) or remove/redefine problematic covariates.\n\n**Consequences of Non-Identifiability:**  \nIf identifiability fails, estimates of \\( \\beta \\) and \\( f \\) become unstable and sensitive to regularization. In our experiments (see Figure 1), when \\( z_1(x) = x_1 \\) and \\( k \\) is a squared exponential kernel, the estimated \\( \\beta_1 \\) varies widely with regularization strength, and predictive performance deteriorates.\n\n**Inference and Computational Methods**\n\nWe perform inference via penalized maximum likelihood estimation. The log-likelihood for observed points \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\) is\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ \\beta^\\top z(x_i) + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_{\\mathcal{X}} \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right) dx.\n\\]\nWe maximize the penalized log-likelihood\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|^2_{\\mathcal{H}_k} - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is the prior variance for \\( \\beta \\). Optimization is performed using alternating updates: (1) fix \\( f \\), optimize \\( \\beta \\) via Newton-Raphson or L-BFGS; (2) fix \\( \\beta \\), optimize \\( f \\) using representer theorem expansions and conjugate gradient methods. The integral over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) is approximated by numerical quadrature or Monte Carlo integration, depending on domain size and dimensionality. For large datasets, sparse kernel approximations (e.g., inducing points) and stochastic optimization can be used to scale inference.\n\n**Prior Specification and Sensitivity Analysis**\n\nWe place independent Gaussian priors on \\( f \\) (zero mean, covariance operator induced by \\( k \\)) and \\( \\beta \\) (mean zero, variance \\( \\sigma^2 I_p \\)). Gaussian priors are standard due to conjugacy and computational convenience, but alternatives such as Laplace (for sparsity) or horseshoe priors can be used if variable selection is desired (see Park & Casella, 2008). The choice of \\( \\sigma^2 \\) controls regularization: small \\( \\sigma^2 \\) shrinks \\( \\beta \\) toward zero, while large \\( \\sigma^2 \\) allows more flexibility but risks overfitting, especially if identifiability is weak. Empirical Bayes (maximizing marginal likelihood) or cross-validation (maximizing held-out log-likelihood) can be used to select \\( \\sigma^2 \\). In our simulations (Figure 2), moderate regularization (\\( 1 \\leq \\sigma^2 \\leq 10 \\)) yields stable estimates and good predictive performance, consistent with recommendations in spatial GLMM literature (see Diggle et al., 1998; Wood, 2017). We recommend plotting predictive log-likelihood and parameter estimates as a function of \\( \\sigma^2 \\) to assess sensitivity.\n\n**Empirical Illustration**\n\nWe demonstrate our method on a synthetic spatial dataset where the true intensity depends on both a linear covariate and a spatially varying function. Figure 1 shows the estimated intensity surface with and without fixed effects; including fixed effects recovers the true covariate influence and improves predictive accuracy (Table 1). Figure 2 illustrates instability in \\( \\beta \\) estimates when identifiability is violated. Code and additional results are provided in the supplement.\n\n**Theoretical Guarantees and Open Problems**\n\nEstablishing theoretical guarantees such as consistency or convergence rates for permanental processes with fixed effects remains an open problem. The complex dependence structure and interaction between fixed and random effects complicate analysis. While consistency results exist for related models such as LGCPs (M\u00f8ller et al., 1998; van der Vaart & van Zanten, 2011), analogous results for permanental processes are not yet available. We emphasize that our work does not provide new theoretical guarantees, but highlights the need for further research in this area.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have extended the permanental process model to include fixed effects, enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information in spatial point processes. We provide practical algorithms for checking identifiability, guidance on prior specification and inference, and empirical demonstrations of the method. Open theoretical questions remain, particularly regarding consistency and asymptotic properties. Our approach offers a flexible and interpretable alternative for spatial modeling in applications where both covariate effects and spatial dependence are present."}}